=== layout: title-content

# Chargaff's Rules

@body

- \[A\] = \[T\]
- \[G\] = \[C\]
- \[A\] + \[G\] = \[T\] + \[C\] = 50%
- \[A\] + \[C\] = \[T\] + \[G\] = 50%
- These rules hold true regardless of the % G+C or the source of the DNA

=== layout: title-content

# Chargaff's Rules

@body

- A sample of dsDNA contains 14% Adenine, what percentages are the other nucleobases?

=== layout: title-content

# Chargaff's Rules

@body

- A sample of dsDNA contains 14% Adenine, what percentages are the other nucleobases?
- \[A\] = 14%
- \[A\] = \[T\]
- so \[T\] = 14%

=== layout: title-content

# Chargaff's Rules

@body

- A sample of dsDNA contains 14% Adenine, what percentages are the other nucleobases?
- \[A\] = \[T\] = 14%
- 100 – 14 – 14 = 72%
- \[G\] + \[C\] = 72%; \[G\] = \[C\]
- 2 \[G\] = 72%; → \[G\] = 36%
- \[G\] = \[C\] = 36%

=== layout: title-content

# Chargaff's Rules

@body

- A sample of dsDNA contains 14% Adenine, what percentages are the other nucleobases?
- \[A\] = \[T\] = 14%
- \[G\] = \[C\] = 36%

